<tr><td>More buses</td><td>The timetable's wait falls in proportion to the buses added, in steps of 10% of the line's buses at its busiest moment up to 40%, then 60%, 80%, 100%, 150%, 200%, 300%, 400%, 600% and 800% more, rounded to whole buses. Two things stop it. One is a bus a minute at the line's busiest stop in its busiest hour, about what one curbside stop can load: the <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/2023/https://onlinepubs.trb.org/onlinepubs/tcrp/tcrp_rpt_165ch-06.pdf#page=21">Transit Capacity and Quality of Service Manual</a> (TCRP Report 165, 3rd edition, p. 6-15) divides the seconds in an hour buses can use the stop by the seconds each one holds it, and at a signal giving buses half the time, its default 10 seconds to pull out and the next bus in, and our assumed 40 second stop, that's 60 an hour. The other is the first step that takes the line's crowded trips to 0%, past which more buses only shorten the wait. A line's busiest moment is the most buses SFMTA's weekday timetable has on it at once, counting the layover between trips (<a href="https://muni-gtfs.apps.sfmta.com/data/muni_gtfs-current.zip">SFMTA GTFS</a>, service from 29 August 2026): ${o.lines["22"]?.peak ? `${o.lines["22"].peak.buses} buses on the 22, at ${o.lines["22"].peak.at}` : "none for the 22"}</td><td>${eff("more", "% of the timetable's wait a bus")}</td><td>Each bus at NTD's cost per vehicle hour for the hours it runs today</td><td>Running cost</td></tr>
